{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EDG8taudF9EFxQYd3c28sA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tLLM agents are increasingly deployed for complex, long-horizon software engineering tasks---resolving GitHub issues, debugging production systems, and iterating on multi-file codebases across dozens of editing rounds \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent,wang2025openhands}.\n5\tAs these deployments scale, a critical failure mode has emerged that is qualitatively different from crashes or syntax errors: the agent \\emph{stagnates}---continuing to produce actions that appear purposeful while making no meaningful progress toward the task goal.\n6\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document this degradation on SlopCodeBench, recording verbosity increases of 77\\% and quality drops of 75.5\\% in long-horizon coding trajectories, with no agent fully solving any benchmark problem end-to-end.\n7\tCrucially, the dominant failure pattern is not simple repetition but what we term \\emph{semantic stagnation}: the agent issues varied commands, edits different files, and generates plausible-looking outputs, yet its behavior is functionally stuck.\n8\tThis ``looks busy but going in circles'' pattern defeats existing monitoring tools, because surface-level action diversity masks the underlying lack of progress.\n9\tWe observe this phenomenon at scale: across trajectories from two architecturally distinct agents---SWE-Agent \\citep{yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands \\citep{wang2025openhands}---on SWE-bench tasks \\citep{jimenez2024swebench}, 28.5\\% and 20.9\\% of sliding windows exhibit stagnation under our silver labeling scheme, respectively, confirming that the failure mode is both prevalent and highly agent-dependent.\n10\t\n11\tExisting approaches address aspects of this problem but fall short in three ways.\n12\tFirst, \\emph{repetition detectors} such as cycle detection \\citep{george2025cycledetection} and SpecRA \\citep{pipis2025reasoningloop} identify structural repetition---exact or near-exact loops in action sequences---but by design miss semantically diverse stagnation where the agent varies its surface behavior while remaining functionally stuck.\n13\tSecond, \\emph{step-level scoring} via process reward models \\citep{xi2025agentprm} evaluates individual actions in isolation, discarding the window-level temporal dynamics that distinguish transient setbacks from sustained stagnation---a distinction that requires reasoning over sequences of consecutive actions \\citep{chen2025monitoring}.\n14\tA sequence of failed commands followed by a strategy switch, for instance, carries different diagnostic information than the same commands evaluated independently.\n15\tThird, \\emph{LLM self-evaluation} \\citep{shinn2023reflexion} is both expensive and fundamentally limited by the stuck model's own judgment; our LLM-as-judge baseline achieves only 0.663 macro-F1 at \\$11.66 per test set, well below lightweight feature-based alternatives.\n16\tMore fundamentally, none of these approaches asks the prior question: \\emph{what signals actually carry stagnation information?}\n17\tNo systematic study has investigated whether detecting subtle agent failure requires sophisticated semantic understanding or whether simpler structural patterns suffice.\n18\t\n19\tWe address this question through a systematic empirical study (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}).\n20\tUsing a weak supervision framework \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}, we construct silver-labeled datasets from over 205K windows spanning SWE-Agent (54,809 windows, 28.5\\% silver-labeled stagnation) and OpenHands (150,652 windows, 20.9\\% stagnation).\n21\tThree labeling functions provide complementary noisy votes: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) targets repetitive behavior, file diff delta ($\\lambda_2$) targets output productivity, and outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) propagates task-level outcomes backward through the trajectory.\n22\tMajority vote aggregation produces window-level binary labels with confidence scores.\n23\tOur window-level formulation ($k{=}5$ consecutive actions, stride 1) captures short-range temporal patterns---such as repeated failures followed by strategy changes---that step-level approaches cannot express, while providing the granularity to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a trajectory, enabling targeted interventions.\n24\tAgainst these silver labels, we systematically compare hand-crafted structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n25\t\n26\tOur study yields three principal findings.\n27\t\n28\t\\textbf{Finding 1: Structural features enable effective zero-data cross-coding-agent transfer, but probes surpass them with fine-tuning.}\n29\tThirty hand-crafted features achieve 0.995 macro-F1 in-domain and 0.967 cross-coding-agent (SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands) with zero target data, outperforming the GRU probe \\citep{cho2014gru} by 36.9\\,pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8\\,pp.\n30\tWith target fine-tuning the probe reaches 0.976, surpassing the structural baseline on all windows (0.967).\n31\tThe in-domain score reflects circular evaluation (\\S\\ref{sec:circular}); the cross-coding-agent score breaks this circularity, as models trained on one agent's LF distribution are tested on another with substantially different firing patterns.\n32\tA feature overlap ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}) confirms that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-coding-agent F1 on all windows, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566---the features that generalize encode universal stagnation signals, not LF artifacts.\n33\tThis suggests that lightweight structural monitoring (no GPU, ${<}$5\\,ms per window) may be more practical than neural or LLM-based approaches for real-time agent oversight.\n34\t\n35\t\\textbf{Finding 2: Label quality, not LF selection, is the dominant cross-coding-agent transfer bottleneck.}\n36\tLabeling functions exhibit systematic temporal dynamics that reverse across agents: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) leads stagnation onset on SWE-Agent by $-1.87$ steps but lags on OpenHands by $+3.32$ steps.\n37\tA label quality audit reveals that fixing OH label version bias---correcting stagnation rate from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%---improves 5\\% fine-tuning F1 from 0.570 (under previous label version) to 0.850.\n38\tPrevalence-matched ablation shows that $\\lambda_1$'s core value is coverage ($-29.4$\\,pp when removed, $p \\approx 0$), not label precision: it fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows with precision 97.91\\% (FP rate 2.3\\%).\n39\t\n40\t\\textbf{Finding 3: No label model improves over majority vote.}\n41\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, lag-aware HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal})---all fail to surpass majority vote on downstream detection.\n42\tTWV achieves marginally higher self-evaluated F1 but degrades downstream probe performance by 2.57\\,pp; Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp).\n43\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication: three binary LFs produce too little inter-LF conflict for any method to exploit.\n44\t\n45\tOur contributions are:\n46\t\\begin{enumerate}\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Weak supervision methodology for agent monitoring.} We design a silver-labeling pipeline (3 LFs + majority vote) over 205K+ windows from two coding agents, enabling systematic comparison of detection methods without manual annotation.\n48\t    \\item \\textbf{Label quality as transfer bottleneck.} We show that labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal reversals and that fixing label version bias (stag rate 6.56\\%$\\to$21.4\\% on the embedding subset) is the dominant factor for cross-coding-agent transfer, informing future weak-supervision design on sequential agent data.\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Label model negative finding.} We show that five label models---three temporal and two generative---fail to improve over majority vote for downstream detection. The bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: the 3-LF regime produces insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to add value.\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Deployment-regime trade-off.} In zero-data settings, 30 structural features outperform the neural probe by 36.9\\,pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8\\,pp on silver labels (no GPU, ${<}$5\\,ms per window). With target-agent fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features on all windows (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967 macro-F1), delineating when structural monitoring suffices and when fine-tuning is warranted.\n51\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"314223a0-2662-4a97-bb2c-91ba0af3ef71","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:44:30.936Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Pdc4W65DE4gk2Bw92LY8dd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K8v7cXQntV3oWvJGu3jnsQ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/method.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7131,"cache_read_input_tokens":9038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7131,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"ca29b649-5e59-4a10-bd9d-2c2c239f21b6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K8v7cXQntV3oWvJGu3jnsQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe frame stagnation detection as window-level binary classification over agent trajectories.\n5\tSilver labels are generated via weak supervision from three labeling functions, then used to train and evaluate three detection approaches: structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n6\tFigure~\\ref{fig:pipeline} provides a system overview.\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_pipeline.pdf}\n11\t\\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}\n12\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n13\t\\end{figure*}\n14\t\n15\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n16\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n17\t\\label{sec:problem}\n18\t\n19\tGiven an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\n20\tThe task is to assign each window a binary label $y_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, where 0 denotes \\emph{stagnation} (the agent is stuck or unproductive) and 1 denotes \\emph{progress} (the agent is making meaningful headway toward the task goal).\n21\t\n22\tThe window formulation offers two advantages over alternatives.\n23\tCompared to step-level scoring, windows capture short-range temporal patterns---such as a sequence of repeated failed attempts---that individual actions cannot express.\n24\tCompared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\n25\t\n26\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}\n28\t\\label{sec:weak_supervision}\n29\t\n30\tPrior gold-standard annotations for agent stagnation at the window level are unavailable; we construct a stratified gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n31\tManual annotation is expensive: each window requires reviewing $k$ actions with full context (command text, outputs, file diffs), taking an estimated 2--3 minutes per window.\n32\tWe therefore adopt a data programming approach \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}: multiple labeling functions (LFs) provide noisy votes that are aggregated into silver labels.\n33\tEach LF $\\lambda_j : w_i \\to \\{0, 1, \\bot\\}$ examines a window and emits a vote: stagnation (0), progress (1), or abstain ($\\bot$).\n34\t\n35\t\\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}\n36\t\\label{sec:labeling_functions}\n37\t\n38\tWe design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation.\n39\t\n40\t\\paragraph{Command repetition ($\\lambda_1$).}\n41\tComputes the proportion of repeated commands within $w_i$; a high ratio triggers a stagnation vote.\n42\tNever abstains.\n43\tFires on 65.5\\% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4\\% of OH windows (97.9\\% precision), reflecting agent-specific temporal dynamics (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{File diff delta ($\\lambda_2$).}\n46\tMeasures the fraction of steps producing code modifications.\n47\tLow modification frequency triggers stagnation; ${>}1$ edited file triggers progress; otherwise abstains.\n48\tOn OH, achieves 87.5\\% recall (99.9\\% precision), the most reliable cross-coding-agent signal.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$).}\n51\tUses the final task outcome as distant supervision: unresolved tasks' last windows receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes; all others abstain.\n52\tUnlike $\\lambda_1$/$\\lambda_2$, $\\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.\n53\t\n54\t\n55\t\\subsubsection{Label Aggregation}\n56\t\\label{sec:label_aggregation}\n57\t\n58\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:\n59\t\\begin{equation}\n60\ty_i = \\text{MV}\\bigl(\\{\\lambda_j(w_i) : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot\\}\\bigr)\n61\t\\label{eq:mv}\n62\t\\end{equation}\n63\tWe define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\\text{conf}_i = |\\{j : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot \\;\\text{and}\\; \\lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\\}| \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$.\n64\tConf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.\n65\tThe conf${\\geq}$2 subset, where at least two non-abstaining LFs agree on the winning label, serves as the primary evaluation set throughout this work.\n66\tThis stratification allows us to separately analyze model behavior on high-confidence vs.\\ ambiguous windows (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n67\t\n68\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n70\t\\label{sec:detection_methods}\n71\t\n72\t\\subsubsection{Structural Features}\n73\t\\label{sec:structural_features}\n74\t\n75\tWe extract a 30-dimensional feature vector from each window, requiring no pretrained model or GPU.\n76\tFeatures span four categories:\n77\t(1)~\\emph{repetition}: exact command repeat ratio, fuzzy match ratio (Levenshtein-based), and unique command ratio;\n78\t(2)~\\emph{file operations}: counts of file creates, modifications, and deletions within the window;\n79\t(3)~\\emph{edit patterns}: edit distances between consecutive actions, capturing how much the agent's behavior changes step-to-step;\n80\t(4)~\\emph{output statistics}: entropy of command outputs and action type distribution features.\n81\tThese features are fed to three classifiers: Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n82\tBecause several structural features overlap with the signals used by the labeling functions (e.g., repetition ratios relate to $\\lambda_1$), structural classifiers can partially reconstruct the LF voting process.\n83\tWe discuss this circularity and its implications in \\S\\ref{sec:main_results}.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsubsection{NL Embedding Probe (ProgressProbe)}\n86\t\\label{sec:progressprobe}\n87\t\n88\tEach action $a_t$ is encoded as a natural language description and embedded using Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} into a 1024-dimensional vector.\n89\tThe $k$ embeddings within a window form a sequence that is processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with hidden size 256 and dropout 0.1.\n90\tAn attention mechanism \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} computes a weighted combination of the GRU hidden states, yielding a single window-level representation.\n91\tThis representation is passed through a fully connected layer with sigmoid activation for binary classification.\n92\tTraining uses binary cross-entropy loss with early stopping (patience 5) on a validation set.\n93\t\n94\tThe GRU architecture is motivated by the sequential nature of actions within a window: the order in which commands are issued (e.g., repeated failures followed by a different approach) carries diagnostic information that bag-of-embeddings methods discard.\n95\tWe also evaluate two simpler embedding baselines to isolate the contribution of temporal modeling: (1)~mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression, and (2)~cosine similarity between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n96\t\n97\t\\subsubsection{LLM-as-Judge Baseline}\n98\t\\label{sec:llm_judge}\n99\t\n100\tWe prompt Claude Sonnet 4.6 in zero-shot mode to classify each window as stagnation or progress, given the textual descriptions of actions within the window.\\footnote{Extended LLM evaluation including chain-of-thought prompting, few-shot examples, and multi-model comparison is reported in Appendix~\\ref{sec:llm_extended}.}\n101\tThe LLM judge establishes a cost-performance reference: at \\$11.66 per test set, its zero-shot F1 of 0.663 demonstrates that stagnation detection is non-trivial even for capable LLMs without task-specific adaptation.\n102\tThis baseline is not used in the silver label pipeline.\n103\t\n104\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n105\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n106\t\\label{sec:temporal_models}\n107\t\n108\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}): $\\lambda_1$ leads by ${\\sim}2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ is near-concurrent, and $\\lambda_3$ lags by ${\\sim}7$ steps.\n109\tThis motivates testing whether temporal aggregation improves over majority vote.\n110\tWe evaluate three temporal label models---Time-Varying Weights (TWV) with Gaussian kernel smoothing, a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} over joint LF patterns, and a Lag-Aware HMM that models each LF's temporal offset---alongside two generative i.i.d.\\ models: FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid} and Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}.\n111\tFull model specifications appear in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_temporal_models}."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"051578ac-9496-4c06-bdbe-614d58105476","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:44:31.880Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Pdc4W65DE4gk2Bw92LY8dd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J53sv2v86S1KdGyv5TEYT4","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/experiments.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7131,"cache_read_input_tokens":9038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7131,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"cc8304c9-432d-45bd-a9a3-41a3f01e009b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J53sv2v86S1KdGyv5TEYT4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\tWe organize results around three questions:\n5\t(1)~which method best detects stagnation across deployment scenarios? (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}--\\S\\ref{sec:robustness});\n6\t(2)~what drives label quality and cross-coding-agent transfer bottlenecks? (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}--\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation});\n7\t(3)~can temporal structure in LFs improve labels? (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n8\tAll reported metrics are evaluated against silver labels derived from majority vote over three labeling functions (\\S\\ref{sec:method}).\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation caveat.}\n11\tStructural features (command repetition ratios, file operation counts, edit distances) are computed from the same signals that the labeling functions use to produce silver labels.\n12\tConsequently, the in-domain structural F1 of 0.995 reflects an upper bound on how well these features can \\emph{reverse-engineer} the labeling functions, not detection accuracy against true ground truth.\n13\tThe cross-coding-agent evaluation on OpenHands (0.967 macro-F1) is non-circular: models trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions are tested on a different agent whose LF firing patterns differ substantially (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n14\tA human-annotated gold set provides additional non-circular evidence (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n15\t\n16\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n17\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n18\t\\label{sec:setup}\n19\t\n20\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n21\tSliding windows of $k{=}5$ consecutive actions are extracted from SWE-Agent (54,809 windows, 28.5\\% stagnation) \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands (150,652 windows, 20.9\\% stagnation) \\citep{wang2025openhands} trajectories on SWE-bench.\n22\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote; confidence equals the count of agreeing LFs (1--3).\n23\tSplits are at the trajectory level (70/15/15\\%); evaluation uses conf${\\geq}$2: $N{=}1{,}824$ (SWE-Agent) and $N{=}43{,}376$ (OH).\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Detection methods.}\n26\t(1)~\\textbf{Structural features}: 30 hand-crafted features (repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances, output entropy) fed to Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n27\t(2)~\\textbf{ProgressProbe}: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} embeddings (1024-dim) processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with attention \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} (hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1), early-stopped at epoch~3 with patience~5.\n28\t(3)~\\textbf{Mean-pool + LR}: Mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression.\n29\t(4)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 4.6 zero-shot window-level classification (\\$11.66 API cost for the SWE test set).\n30\t(5)~\\textbf{Cosine similarity}: Cosine distance between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n31\t\n32\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n33\tMacro-F1 is the primary metric. We also report per-class F1 for stagnation (F1\\textsubscript{stag}) and progress (F1\\textsubscript{prog}).\n34\t\n35\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n36\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n37\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n38\t\n39\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents in-domain results on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set and cross-coding-agent transfer to OpenHands.\n40\t\n41\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n42\t\\centering\n43\t\\small\n44\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}\n45\t\\toprule\n46\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{SWE-Agent In-Domain} (conf${\\geq}$2)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{OpenHands Transfer}} \\\\\n47\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n48\t\\textbf{Method} & Macro-F1 & F1\\textsubscript{stag} & F1\\textsubscript{prog} & All & Conf${\\geq}$2 \\\\\n49\t\\midrule\n50\tStructural RF$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & 0.9123 & \\textbf{0.9959} \\\\\n51\tStructural GB$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & \\textbf{0.9674} & \\underline{0.9918} \\\\\n52\tStructural LR$^\\dagger$       & 0.9874 & 0.9815 & 0.9933 & 0.7801 & 0.9671 \\\\\n53\tProgressProbe (GRU)           & \\underline{0.9055} & 0.8593 & 0.9518 & 0.598$^a$ & 0.598$^a$ \\\\\n54\t\\quad + full OH fine-tune      & ---    & ---    & ---    & 0.976$^b$ & 0.976$^b$ \\\\\n55\tMean-pool + LR                & 0.8435 & 0.7620 & 0.9250 & ---    & --- \\\\\n56\tLLM-as-Judge                  & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & ---    & --- \\\\\n57\tCosine Similarity             & 0.5606 & ---    & ---    & 0.447  & 0.393 \\\\\n58\t\\bottomrule\n59\t\\end{tabular}\n60\t\\caption{Stagnation detection results (SWE-Agent in-domain, $N{=}1{,}824$; OpenHands zero-shot transfer). $^\\dagger$Structural metrics reflect LF-reconstructibility, not ground-truth accuracy (\\S\\ref{sec:circular}). $^a$Probe evaluated on 15\\% test split ($N{=}6{,}122$); structural models on all OH windows ($N{=}150{,}652$) and conf${\\geq}$2 ($N{=}43{,}376$). $^b$Paired bootstrap ($N{=}6{,}122$): GB leads fine-tuned probe by 1.65\\,pp (CI [1.11, 2.23], $p{<}0.001$) on conf${\\geq}$2; probe advantage on all windows reflects better conf${=}1$ performance. \\textbf{Bold} = best, \\underline{underline} = second.}\n61\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n62\t\\end{table*}\n63\t\n64\tIn-domain, structural RF achieves 0.995 macro-F1, exceeding the GRU probe by 8.96\\,pp (paired bootstrap: 95\\% CI [7.44, 10.57]\\,pp, $p{<}0.001$).\n65\tHowever, the structural--probe gap is inflated by circular evaluation: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels.\n66\tThe more informative comparison is among non-circular methods: the GRU probe outperforms mean-pool~+~LR by 6.2\\,pp, confirming that temporal modeling within windows adds value beyond static embedding aggregation.\n67\tThe zero-shot LLM judge (0.663) underperforms all learned methods despite substantially higher cost (\\$11.66 per test set).\n68\tExtended evaluation with chain-of-thought and few-shot prompting (Appendix~\\ref{sec:llm_extended}) yields a best LLM macro-F1 of 0.799 (five-shot-CoT, SWE-Agent in-domain), still 16.8\\,pp below structural cross-coding-agent transfer (0.967 on OpenHands)---the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-coding-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime.\n69\tCoT alone yields gains comparable to few-shot ($+$8.7\\,pp vs.\\ $+$8.9\\,pp over zero-shot); combining them (five-shot-CoT) adds a further $+$4.8\\,pp, suggesting complementary mechanisms.\n70\tNotably, providing the 30 structural features as additional LLM input degrades performance from 0.751 (few-shot) to 0.688, suggesting the signal resides in engineered feature interactions inaccessible to natural language reasoning (Appendix~\\ref{sec:llm_extended}).\n71\t\n72\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n73\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n74\t\\label{sec:cross_agent}\n75\t\n76\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} (right columns) shows models trained on SWE-Agent and evaluated on OpenHands without any OH training data.\n77\tStructural GB transfers best, achieving 0.967 macro-F1 on all OH windows.\n78\tRF drops to 0.912 on all-confidence OH data despite matching GB in-domain, suggesting GB's boosted ensemble is more robust to distributional shift.\n79\tBoth structural models exceed 0.99 macro-F1 on conf${\\geq}$2 OH windows (GB 0.992, RF 0.996), where label noise is lower.\n80\t\n81\tThe probe's zero-shot transfer (0.598) lags structural GB by 36.9\\,pp---cosine similarity collapses further to 0.447 (mean cosine distance between stagnation and progress windows is 0.610 vs.\\ 0.613, showing essentially no class separation)---but full OH fine-tuning closes this gap dramatically: the fine-tuned probe reaches 0.976 macro-F1 on all OH windows, surpassing structural GB (0.967) by 0.9\\,pp (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n82\tThe asymmetry between structural and learned methods is thus stage-dependent: in the zero-data regime, structural features transfer naturally while learned embeddings do not (+36.9\\,pp gap); with target-domain fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features (+0.9\\,pp on all windows).\n83\tThe practical implication is a deployment trade-off: use structural features when no labeled data is available; invest in probe fine-tuning when annotation budget permits.\n84\tHowever, this crossover is scope-dependent: on conf${\\geq}$2 windows, where label noise is minimal, GB retains its advantage (0.992 vs.\\ 0.976; paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$, $N{=}6{,}122$), suggesting that the probe's all-windows edge reflects better tolerance of noisy low-confidence labels rather than superior high-confidence detection.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}\n87\tStructural RF: $0.995{\\pm}0.000$ in-domain (3 seeds); GB cross-coding-agent: $0.968{\\pm}0.002$.\n88\tThe probe shows higher variance ($0.919{\\pm}0.011$) but all seeds exceed 0.90.\n89\t\n90\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\n92\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n93\t\n94\tThe probe is encoder-agnostic: three backbones all exceed 0.90 F1 (Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:encoder_ablation}), and a Transformer encoder is equivalent to GRU ($+$0.18\\,pp, $p{=}0.82$) on 5-step windows.\n95\tWindow size $k{=}5$ is optimal; $k{=}3$ drops 5.9\\,pp, $k{=}7$ drops 1.8\\,pp (Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:window_ablation}).\n96\tStructural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\\% training data, fully saturating by 50\\%---a 20$\\times$ advantage over the probe (Figure~\\ref{fig:data_scaling}).\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n99\t\\centering\n100\t\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}\n101\t\\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers saturate by 50\\%; the probe has not plateaued at 100\\%.}\n102\t\\label{fig:data_scaling}\n103\t\\end{figure}\n104\t\n105\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n106\t\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\n107\t\\label{sec:lf_temporal}\n108\t\n109\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_temporal}, Figure~\\ref{fig:lf_temporal} in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_lf_temporal}): cmd\\_rep leads by 1.87 steps, file\\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), and outcome\\_bp lags by 6.70 steps (all pairwise $p{<}10^{-26}$).\n110\tOn OpenHands, cmd\\_rep reverses to lagging ($+3.32$ steps), firing on only 5.4\\% of windows with 97.9\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall detector whose temporal role differs fundamentally from its SWE-Agent behavior.\n111\t\n112\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n113\t\\subsection{Label Quality and cmd\\_rep Coverage}\n114\t\\label{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}\n115\t\n116\tA label quality audit revealed a version mismatch in OH embedding vectors; after correction, the OH stagnation rate shifted from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%, and 5\\% fine-tuning F1 improved from 0.570 to 0.850---demonstrating that label quality, not model capacity, was the transfer bottleneck (full curve in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep}).\n117\t\n118\t\\begin{table}[t]\n119\t\\centering\n120\t\\small\n121\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n122\t\\toprule\n123\t\\textbf{LF Config} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} \\\\\n124\t\\midrule\n125\t3-LF (w/ cmd\\_rep) & \\textbf{0.9763} & 0.9600 & 0.9930 \\\\\n126\t2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) & 0.6822 & 0.4810 & 0.8840 \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\t$\\Delta$ & $-29.4$\\,pp & $-47.9$\\,pp & $-10.9$\\,pp \\\\\n129\t\\bottomrule\n130\t\\end{tabular}\n131\t\\caption{Prevalence-matched ablation on the same OH test set ($N{=}6{,}122$, stag rate 19.3\\%). Removing cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$).}\n132\t\\label{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}\n133\t\\end{table}\n134\t\n135\tRemoving cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (Table~\\ref{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}; McNemar $p{\\approx}0$) due to data volume collapse: cmd\\_rep expands training data ${\\sim}$10$\\times$ (4K$\\to$43K windows) while agreeing 100\\% with 2-LF labels on overlapping windows---purely a coverage contribution (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_label_quality}).\n136\t\n137\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n138\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n139\t\\label{sec:temporal_label_models}\n140\t\n141\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM, Lag-HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL)---all fail to improve over majority vote (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_models} in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_tlm_results}); the best (TWV, $+$0.6\\,pp self-evaluated) degrades downstream probe F1 by 2.57\\,pp.\n142\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity: three binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns with insufficient inter-LF conflict to exploit.\n143\t\n144\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\\subsection{Gold Set Validation}\n146\t\\label{sec:gold_set}\n147\tThree CS graduate students annotate a stratified gold set; 512 windows received at least one annotation, with Set~B ($N{=}505$, ${\\geq}$2 annotators agree) as the primary evaluation (protocol and IAA in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_gold_set}).\n148\tGB achieves 0.839 gold macro-F1 vs.\\ 0.798 for MV silver labels ($+$4.2\\,pp, paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$), confirming that structural features detect stagnation beyond LF reconstruction.\n149\tProbe models are evaluated on the SWE-only subset ($N{\\approx}335$) due to missing OH embeddings; cross-method comparisons should account for differing $N$ (Table~\\ref{tab:gold_batch1})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"c478c7cc-2c3a-420b-9864-58a5cd500838","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:44:32.017Z"}
