{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YXwiJhd6ci1YPbXUNrZ8RA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KsWUhu5SM48r8Ajmu6byUq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**. Review the paper below thoroughly and independently. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\"\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agents — prolonged sequences of locally plausible but collectively unproductive actions. It proposes a weak supervision framework using three labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff activity (file_diff), and outcome backpropagation (OB). Structural features (30 lightweight features, <5ms per window) are trained on these silver labels via Gradient Boosted Trees (GB) and Random Forest (RF).\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Zero-shot structural dominance: GB achieves 0.967 cross-agent F1 on OpenHands (trained on SWE-Agent), outperforming probes by +33.4pp (matched-scope) and LLM judges by +20.5pp.\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis: LFs have agent-specific temporal roles; label quality (not model architecture) is the primary cross-agent transfer bottleneck; circularity quantified (in-domain 0.995 F1).\n3. Temporal label model (TLM) negative result: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to improve over majority vote.\n\n**Additional analyses:**\n- Compute waste: 13.7% in stagnation tails, only 7.8% truly wasted after accounting for recovery bursts (43.5% of tails contain recovery)\n- Early termination simulation: No k achieves >15% savings with >90% task preservation\n- Pareto analysis: Structural AUC=0.56, only +13pp above random\n- 2-LF deployable config: collapses cross-agent (2.81% coverage on OH)\n\n**Related work covered in paper:**\n- Agent failure detection: CDCS, CDSA (Zhang et al. 2024), SpecRA, TrajAD, SlopCodeBench (2026)\n- Process reward models: AgentPRM (Xi et al. 2026) — TD/GAE step-level reward\n- LLM self-evaluation: Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023), LATS (Zhou et al. 2024), Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al. 2024)\n- Agent monitoring: ICLR Workshop Authors (2025), Monitoring Authors (2025)\n- Weak supervision: Ratner et al. (2016) data programming\n\n**Paper structure:** 8 main pages + 3 pages appendix. 12 tables total. No figures. 10 references.\n\n**Key experimental setup:**\n- Data: SWE-Agent (543 trajectories) + OpenHands (3,409 trajectories) = 143K windows\n- Cross-agent: Train on SWE-Agent, evaluate zero-shot on OpenHands\n- Gold set: 491 windows, 3 annotators, κ=0.42 on SWE subset\n- LLM baselines: Claude (zero-shot, few-shot-CoT), GPT-5.5 (zero-shot) on 2,048-window OH subset\n- Matched-scope evaluation: prevalence-matched OH subset (N=6,122, 19.3% stagnation)\n\n**Review context notes:**\n- The paper originally aimed to include intervention (early termination) as a 4th claim, but this failed — stagnation is intermittent, making consecutive-k intervention fundamentally limited\n- The paper pivoted to a \"3-claim detection paper\" with intervention data as motivation\n- The 2-LF \"deployable\" configuration is acknowledged as non-deployable cross-agent\n- Gold set shows 20pp drop from in-domain F1 (0.995→0.792 on SWE), revealing circularity dependence\n\n**Your task:** Provide a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focusing on novelty, positioning, significance)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focusing on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning/framing issues, whether contributions are truly novel or incremental)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Related work gaps** (specific missing references or comparisons)\n6. **Minor issues**\n7. **Overall assessment** (score with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is \"semantic stagnation detection\" genuinely novel or a repackaging of known anomaly detection? Are the LFs (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) novel or obvious heuristics? Is the weak supervision framing adding value or overcomplicating simple feature engineering? Does the negative TLM result constitute a genuine contribution? How does the 20pp circularity gap affect the paper's claims? Is the paper trying to do too many things (detection + TLM + compute waste + intervention) without excelling at any? With only 10 references, is the related work adequate?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14366,"cache_read_input_tokens":36993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14366},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"5549513c-e14c-46b8-a599-89a182b8821e"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a5a3401a60941d030","tool_use_id":"toolu_01KsWUhu5SM48r8Ajmu6byUq","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**. Review the paper below thoroughly and independently. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\"\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agents — prolonged sequences of locally plausible but collectively unproductive actions. It proposes a weak supervision framework using three labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff activity (file_diff), and outcome backpropagation (OB). Structural features (30 lightweight features, <5ms per window) are trained on these silver labels via Gradient Boosted Trees (GB) and Random Forest (RF).\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Zero-shot structural dominance: GB achieves 0.967 cross-agent F1 on OpenHands (trained on SWE-Agent), outperforming probes by +33.4pp (matched-scope) and LLM judges by +20.5pp.\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis: LFs have agent-specific temporal roles; label quality (not model architecture) is the primary cross-agent transfer bottleneck; circularity quantified (in-domain 0.995 F1).\n3. Temporal label model (TLM) negative result: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to improve over majority vote.\n\n**Additional analyses:**\n- Compute waste: 13.7% in stagnation tails, only 7.8% truly wasted after accounting for recovery bursts (43.5% of tails contain recovery)\n- Early termination simulation: No k achieves >15% savings with >90% task preservation\n- Pareto analysis: Structural AUC=0.56, only +13pp above random\n- 2-LF deployable config: collapses cross-agent (2.81% coverage on OH)\n\n**Related work covered in paper:**\n- Agent failure detection: CDCS, CDSA (Zhang et al. 2024), SpecRA, TrajAD, SlopCodeBench (2026)\n- Process reward models: AgentPRM (Xi et al. 2026) — TD/GAE step-level reward\n- LLM self-evaluation: Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023), LATS (Zhou et al. 2024), Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al. 2024)\n- Agent monitoring: ICLR Workshop Authors (2025), Monitoring Authors (2025)\n- Weak supervision: Ratner et al. (2016) data programming\n\n**Paper structure:** 8 main pages + 3 pages appendix. 12 tables total. No figures. 10 references.\n\n**Key experimental setup:**\n- Data: SWE-Agent (543 trajectories) + OpenHands (3,409 trajectories) = 143K windows\n- Cross-agent: Train on SWE-Agent, evaluate zero-shot on OpenHands\n- Gold set: 491 windows, 3 annotators, κ=0.42 on SWE subset\n- LLM baselines: Claude (zero-shot, few-shot-CoT), GPT-5.5 (zero-shot) on 2,048-window OH subset\n- Matched-scope evaluation: prevalence-matched OH subset (N=6,122, 19.3% stagnation)\n\n**Review context notes:**\n- The paper originally aimed to include intervention (early termination) as a 4th claim, but this failed — stagnation is intermittent, making consecutive-k intervention fundamentally limited\n- The paper pivoted to a \"3-claim detection paper\" with intervention data as motivation\n- The 2-LF \"deployable\" configuration is acknowledged as non-deployable cross-agent\n- Gold set shows 20pp drop from in-domain F1 (0.995→0.792 on SWE), revealing circularity dependence\n\n**Your task:** Provide a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focusing on novelty, positioning, significance)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focusing on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning/framing issues, whether contributions are truly novel or incremental)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Related work gaps** (specific missing references or comparisons)\n6. **Minor issues**\n7. **Overall assessment** (score with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is \"semantic stagnation detection\" genuinely novel or a repackaging of known anomaly detection? Are the LFs (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) novel or obvious heuristics? Is the weak supervision framing adding value or overcomplicating simple feature engineering? Does the negative TLM result constitute a genuine contribution? How does the 20pp circularity gap affect the paper's claims? Is the paper trying to do too many things (detection + TLM + compute waste + intervention) without excelling at any? With only 10 references, is the related work adequate?","uuid":"48830dc6-b031-4111-bf74-a0ac4d599edc","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a"}
