{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CeSkggptN9gSpARBPABS1z","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review.\n\n---\n\n## REVIEW: Reviewer 1 (Methodology & Experiments)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) aggregated by majority vote to produce silver labels over 205K+ windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands. The core finding is that 30 hand-crafted structural features trained on these silver labels achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 F1) without target data, outperforming neural probes and LLM judges, though the probe surpasses structural features with fine-tuning. Secondary contributions include temporal dynamics analysis of LFs, a label quality transfer bottleneck finding, and a negative result showing five label models fail to beat majority vote.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Honest circularity disclosure.** The paper is commendably upfront that the in-domain 0.995 F1 reflects LF reconstruction, not ground-truth detection. The circular evaluation caveat at the top of Section 4 is well-placed and the paper systematically attempts to break this circularity through cross-agent evaluation, instance isolation, and gold validation. This level of self-awareness is rare and appreciated.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer is the right evaluation.** The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands transfer setup (0.967 GB) is the paper's strongest evidence. The fact that cmd_rep fires on 65.5% of SWE windows but only 5.4% of OH windows means a model that merely memorizes LF outputs would fail dramatically, yet GB succeeds. Instance isolation (Table 9, <0.1pp delta) further rules out task-level memorization.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is informative.** Table 10 showing that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent (exceeding the full 30-feature model) while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566 is a genuinely interesting finding. The leave-one-group-out ablation localizing signal to cmd_rep and file_diff features is methodologically sound.\n\n- **Thorough negative result on label models.** Testing five label models across two families (temporal and generative) and showing uniform failure with clear root cause analysis (insufficient inter-LF conflict from only 3 binary LFs) is a well-characterized negative contribution. The TWV case where self-evaluated F1 improves (+0.6pp) but downstream performance degrades (-2.57pp) is a useful cautionary finding.\n\n- **Practical considerations well-addressed.** The <5ms per window, no-GPU requirement for structural features, the data efficiency curve (0.987 F1 at 10% training data), and the explicit deployment trade-off framing (structural for zero-data, probe for fine-tuned) give practitioners actionable guidance.\n\n- **Statistical rigor in reported comparisons.** Bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, multi-seed stability, and Kruskal-Wallis with Bonferroni correction are used throughout. The paper does not rely on point estimates alone.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The entire evaluation edifice rests on silver labels of unknown quality.** Tables 1-5, which contain all headline numbers, evaluate against silver labels produced by the same 3 LFs that the structural features partially reconstruct. The gold set (N=505, Fleiss kappa=0.39) is the only non-circular ground truth, yet it is small and shows only moderate agreement. On the gold set, GB achieves 0.839---a 15.6pp drop from the silver-evaluated cross-agent 0.995 (conf>=2) and a 12.8pp drop from the all-windows 0.967. The paper's headline numbers (0.967, +33.4pp, +16.8pp) are all silver-evaluated. The gold set numbers (0.839 vs. 0.798 MV, +4.2pp) are far less dramatic. The paper should be honest that the practical advantage over majority vote on real labels is 4.2pp, not 16.8pp or 33.4pp. This is a fundamental mismatch between the abstract's claims and the ground-truth evidence.\n\n- **W2: Gold set is underpowered and agreement is concerning.** Fleiss kappa=0.39 is \"fair agreement\"---barely above chance for a binary task. The SWE-only subset (N~335) shows kappa=0.42. This means the annotators themselves cannot reliably agree on what constitutes stagnation, which undermines the gold set as a validation instrument. With only 52 three-annotator windows (Set A), the IAA estimate itself has wide confidence intervals. The gold set comparison of GB (0.877) vs. TWV Probe (0.854) on SWE-only yields p=0.14---not significant---yet the paper still claims structural dominance. The gold set needed to be 3-5x larger and have higher agreement to support the paper's claims.\n\n- **W3: The \"zero-shot dominance\" claim compares apples to oranges.** The headline \"+16.8pp over best LLM judge\" compares structural GB cross-agent on OH (0.967, silver-evaluated) against Claude 5-shot-CoT on SWE in-domain (0.799, also silver-evaluated). This is not a matched comparison: the structural model is evaluated cross-agent on 150K OH windows while the LLM is evaluated in-domain on 1,824 SWE windows. The paper acknowledges the LLM was not run on OH (\"---\" in Table 1), but headlines the comparison anyway. A fair comparison would require running the LLM judge on OH windows. The \"+33.4pp over probes\" comparison (0.952 vs. 0.618) is matched-scope but the 0.952 is the matched-scope structural number, not the cross-agent 0.967---still, this comparison is less problematic since both are evaluated on the same test set.\n\n- **W4: Outcome backpropagation (lambda_3) is a privileged signal that cannot be used at deployment time.** The paper acknowledges this in the limitations but does not sufficiently address the implications for the headline claims. lambda_3 contributes 33.5% of conf>=2 labels. Removing it drops performance by 7.7pp. The 2-LF deployable configuration (without lambda_3 and without cmd_rep on OH) collapses to 2.81% coverage cross-agent (Table 5 in the task description). This means the \"deployable\" version of this system covers almost no windows on a new agent. The paper buries this critical finding rather than confronting it head-on.\n\n- **W5: The probe comparison is unfair due to evaluation scope mismatch.** Table 1 footnote (a) reveals that the probe is evaluated on a 15% test split (N=6,122) while structural models are evaluated on ALL OH windows (N=150,652). This is a 24.6x difference in evaluation set size. The structural models see windows that the probe was trained on (in the fine-tuned setting) or that overlap with its training distribution. The paper should have restricted structural model evaluation to the same N=6,122 test split for a fair head-to-head.\n\n- **W6: The paper evolved from a failed intervention study.** The review context reveals that the original Claim 4 (early termination as a practical intervention) failed: no k achieves >15% savings with >90% task preservation, the best operating point saves only 7.6% compute with 18.5% false termination, and 43.5% of \"stagnation tails\" contain recovery. The paper pivoted to a \"3-claim detection paper\" but the ghost of the intervention story lingers in the framing (\"compute waste,\" \"deployment trade-off\"). The paper would be more honest as \"we can detect stagnation but cannot usefully act on it.\"\n\n- **W7: Only two agents from the same domain tested.** Both SWE-Agent and OpenHands operate on SWE-bench (GitHub issue resolution). Cross-agent transfer within a single domain and benchmark is a weaker claim than the paper implies. The agents share the same task distribution, success criteria, and output format. True generalization would require testing on web navigation, data analysis, or multi-agent systems.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gold-silver concordance:** What is the agreement rate between silver labels and gold labels on the 505-window gold set? If silver labels are 80% accurate against gold (implied by MV's 0.798 gold F1), then all silver-evaluated metrics in Tables 1-5 have ~20% label noise. How do you reconcile headline claims (0.967) with gold-validated performance (0.839)?\n\n2. **LLM judge on OpenHands:** Why was the LLM judge not evaluated on OH windows? This is the only setting where circularity is broken, yet the most expensive baseline is absent. Even a subsample evaluation would strengthen the comparison.\n\n3. **2-LF deployable coverage collapse:** Table 5 shows 2-LF conf>=2 coverage drops to 2.81% on OH cross-agent. Given that lambda_3 requires outcome labels (unavailable at inference), what is the actual deployable coverage of this system on a new agent?\n\n4. **Stagnation definition validity:** With kappa=0.39, annotators disagree on ~30% of windows. Have you considered that \"semantic stagnation\" may not be a well-defined binary construct? Could a continuous progress score be more appropriate?\n\n5. **Feature overlap interpretation:** The LF-overlap features (11) outperform the full set (30) cross-agent by 1.0pp. Does this not suggest that the structural classifier is simply learning a better version of the LF voting function, rather than detecting stagnation per se?\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **LLM judge evaluation on OpenHands.** The paper's headline comparison (structural 0.967 vs. LLM 0.799) is cross-domain vs. in-domain. Running even Claude zero-shot on a subsample of OH would establish whether the LLM's cross-agent transfer is comparable to the structural model's.\n\n- **Gold-evaluated cross-agent transfer.** The gold set should include sufficient OH windows with gold labels to evaluate cross-agent GB performance against human ground truth, not just silver labels. The current gold set has OH windows but the probe cannot be evaluated on them due to \"missing OH embeddings\"---this should have been fixed.\n\n- **Ablation of lambda_3 on cross-agent transfer.** The paper reports that removing lambda_3 costs 7.7pp in-domain but does not report the cross-agent transfer performance of a 2-LF (cmd_rep + file_diff only) structural model. This is the only practically deployable configuration.\n\n- **Stagnation-conditioned task outcome analysis.** If structural features detect stagnation at 0.839 gold F1, does acting on this detection (e.g., early termination, strategy reset) actually improve task completion rates? Without downstream task impact, detection accuracy is necessary but not sufficient for the claimed practical value.\n\n- **Calibration analysis.** The structural classifiers output probabilities. Are these well-calibrated? A reliability diagram would be informative, especially given the silver label noise.\n\n- **Sensitivity to window size k.** The ablation (Table 3 appendix) only tests k in {3,5,7} for the probe. The structural features should also be ablated across window sizes, since the LFs are computed at the window level and k=5 was co-designed with the LFs.\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper title in main.tex (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\") differs from the title given in the review prompt. The authors should pick one.\n- Table 1 uses bold/underline conventions but the in-domain column shows both RF and GB at 0.9951 as bold, while the cross-agent columns bold different models. The convention is inconsistent.\n- The label encoding (0=stagnation, 1=progress) is counter-intuitive for a \"stagnation detection\" paper where stagnation is the positive class. Section 3.1 defines this but subsequent text switches between conventions (the error analysis in the appendix explicitly notes \"stagnation-positive convention\").\n- \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\" and \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\" and \"GPT-5.5\" are used but only Qwen3 is cited. The other models should have citations or version identifiers.\n- The claim that structural features require \"<5ms per window\" is not benchmarked or cited. A simple timing table would suffice.\n- The GPT-5.5 evaluation has 22.4% parse failures (408/1,824), making it an unreliable baseline. The paper computes F1 over only valid outputs, which inflates the score.\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant problem (detecting when LLM agents are stuck) and demonstrates commendable transparency about circularity. The weak supervision methodology is sound, the feature overlap ablation is well-designed, and the negative label model result is genuinely useful.\n\nHowever, the paper's headline claims do not survive scrutiny against its own evidence. The central tension is: all impressive numbers (0.967, +33.4pp, +16.8pp) are silver-evaluated, while gold-validated performance (0.839, +4.2pp over MV) is modest with low annotator agreement (kappa=0.39). The \"zero-shot dominance\" comparison is not apples-to-apples (cross-agent structural vs. in-domain LLM). The practically deployable configuration (2-LF without outcome labels) collapses to near-zero coverage on a new agent. The paper reads as \"structural features can reconstruct labeling functions well, and this reconstruction transfers across agents\"---a less exciting but more accurate framing than \"structural features dominate stagnation detection.\"\n\nThe work has the ingredients of a solid empirical study but needs: (1) a much larger gold set with higher agreement to anchor the headline claims, (2) matched-scope comparisons throughout (especially running LLM judges on OH), (3) honest confrontation of the deployability gap (lambda_3 requires outcomes, 2-LF collapses cross-agent), and (4) reframing claims to match what the gold evidence actually supports. As currently written, the gap between advertised and validated performance is too large for a top venue.\n\n### 8. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I have read the full paper including appendices, verified the experimental setup against the review context, and checked internal consistency of reported numbers. I am confident in my assessment of the circularity, gold set limitations, and comparison fairness issues. My uncertainty is around whether the cross-agent transfer result (0.967) genuinely reflects stagnation detection rather than LF reconstruction---the feature overlap ablation provides evidence for the former, but the gold set is too small to be definitive."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6a23cb02bba26923 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6a23cb02bba26923' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 51446\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 141135</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"ff35989b-f839-4026-b635-ea5f70fbb600","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:46:48.333Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methodology and experiments**. Review the paper below thoroughly and independently. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\"\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM coding agents — prolonged sequences of locally plausible but collectively unproductive actions. It proposes a weak supervision framework using three labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff activity (file_diff), and outcome backpropagation (OB). Structural features (30 lightweight features, <5ms per window) are trained on these silver labels via Gradient Boosted Trees (GB) and Random Forest (RF).\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Zero-shot structural dominance: GB achieves 0.967 cross-agent F1 on OpenHands (trained on SWE-Agent), outperforming probes by +33.4pp (matched-scope) and LLM judges by +20.5pp.\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis: LFs have agent-specific temporal roles; label quality (not model architecture) is the primary cross-agent transfer bottleneck; circularity quantified (in-domain 0.995 F1 = near-perfect LF reconstruction).\n3. Temporal label model (TLM) negative result: TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM all fail to improve over majority vote.\n\n**Additional analyses (not framed as core claims):**\n- Compute waste analysis: 13.7% of compute in \"stagnation tails\" but 43.5% contain recovery; only 7.8% truly wasted.\n- Early termination simulation: No k achieves >15% savings with >90% task preservation. Best operating point k=3 saves 7.6% with 18.5% false termination.\n- Pareto analysis: Structural AUC=0.56, only +13pp above random (0.43), far from oracle (0.87).\n- 2-LF deployable config: 0.978 in-domain but collapses to 2.81% coverage on OpenHands cross-agent.\n\n**Key tables/results:**\n- Table 1: Matched-scope GB=0.952 vs Probe=0.618 (+33.4pp), 95% CI [29.8, 37.2], p<0.001\n- Table 2: LLM judges on OH: Claude few-shot-CoT 0.762, GPT-5.5 0.698, Structural GB 0.967\n- Table 3: Cross-agent consistency: Structural GB drops only 2.8pp (SWE→OH), Claude FS-CoT drops 3.7pp\n- Table 4: Gold set (491 windows, 3 annotators): GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 (+4.2pp, p<0.001). SWE subset: GB 0.792 vs MV 0.765 (p=0.034). Inter-annotator κ=0.42 on SWE.\n- Table 5: 2-LF cross-agent: coverage collapses from 30.3% (3-LF) to 2.81% (2-LF conf≥2)\n- Table 6: Stagnation tail decomposition: 13.7% tail, 43.5% recovery, 7.8% true waste\n- Table 7: Early termination: k=3 saves 7.6% compute, 18.5% false termination\n- Table 8: Pareto AUC: Oracle 0.87, Structural 0.56, Random 0.43\n- Table 9-12: Appendix per-agent breakdowns and bootstrap CIs\n\n**Experiment design context (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe review context reveals the paper is aware of these weaknesses:\n- Stagnation is intermittent, not terminal — recovery bursts undermine the \"compute waste\" narrative\n- Early termination faces a fundamental precision-recall dilemma with no good operating point\n- 2-LF deployable config is non-deployable cross-agent (cmd_rep fires only 5.39% on OH)\n- The paper pivoted to a \"3-claim detection paper\" after intervention results (Claim 4) failed\n- Gold set on SWE shows 20pp drop from in-domain (0.995→0.792), exposing LF label dependence\n\n**Your task:** Provide a detailed review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with specifics)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specifics — focus on methodology, experimental design, statistical rigor, circularity concerns, evaluation fairness)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**\n6. **Minor issues**\n7. **Overall assessment** (score with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: circularity of evaluation, gold set size and annotator agreement, whether the \"zero-shot dominance\" claim survives scrutiny, whether negative results are well-characterized enough to be contributions, statistical rigor of comparisons, and practical significance.","agentId":"a6a23cb02bba26923","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my detailed review.\n\n---\n\n## REVIEW: Reviewer 1 (Methodology & Experiments)\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) aggregated by majority vote to produce silver labels over 205K+ windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands. The core finding is that 30 hand-crafted structural features trained on these silver labels achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 F1) without target data, outperforming neural probes and LLM judges, though the probe surpasses structural features with fine-tuning. Secondary contributions include temporal dynamics analysis of LFs, a label quality transfer bottleneck finding, and a negative result showing five label models fail to beat majority vote.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Honest circularity disclosure.** The paper is commendably upfront that the in-domain 0.995 F1 reflects LF reconstruction, not ground-truth detection. The circular evaluation caveat at the top of Section 4 is well-placed and the paper systematically attempts to break this circularity through cross-agent evaluation, instance isolation, and gold validation. This level of self-awareness is rare and appreciated.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer is the right evaluation.** The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands transfer setup (0.967 GB) is the paper's strongest evidence. The fact that cmd_rep fires on 65.5% of SWE windows but only 5.4% of OH windows means a model that merely memorizes LF outputs would fail dramatically, yet GB succeeds. Instance isolation (Table 9, <0.1pp delta) further rules out task-level memorization.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is informative.** Table 10 showing that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent (exceeding the full 30-feature model) while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566 is a genuinely interesting finding. The leave-one-group-out ablation localizing signal to cmd_rep and file_diff features is methodologically sound.\n\n- **Thorough negative result on label models.** Testing five label models across two families (temporal and generative) and showing uniform failure with clear root cause analysis (insufficient inter-LF conflict from only 3 binary LFs) is a well-characterized negative contribution. The TWV case where self-evaluated F1 improves (+0.6pp) but downstream performance degrades (-2.57pp) is a useful cautionary finding.\n\n- **Practical considerations well-addressed.** The <5ms per window, no-GPU requirement for structural features, the data efficiency curve (0.987 F1 at 10% training data), and the explicit deployment trade-off framing (structural for zero-data, probe for fine-tuned) give practitioners actionable guidance.\n\n- **Statistical rigor in reported comparisons.** Bootstrap CIs, McNemar tests, multi-seed stability, and Kruskal-Wallis with Bonferroni correction are used throughout. The paper does not rely on point estimates alone.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The entire evaluation edifice rests on silver labels of unknown quality.** Tables 1-5, which contain all headline numbers, evaluate against silver labels produced by the same 3 LFs that the structural features partially reconstruct. The gold set (N=505, Fleiss kappa=0.39) is the only non-circular ground truth, yet it is small and shows only moderate agreement. On the gold set, GB achieves 0.839---a 15.6pp drop from the silver-evaluated cross-agent 0.995 (conf>=2) and a 12.8pp drop from the all-windows 0.967. The paper's headline numbers (0.967, +33.4pp, +16.8pp) are all silver-evaluated. The gold set numbers (0.839 vs. 0.798 MV, +4.2pp) are far less dramatic. The paper should be honest that the practical advantage over majority vote on real labels is 4.2pp, not 16.8pp or 33.4pp. This is a fundamental mismatch between the abstract's claims and the ground-truth evidence.\n\n- **W2: Gold set is underpowered and agreement is concerning.** Fleiss kappa=0.39 is \"fair agreement\"---barely above chance for a binary task. The SWE-only subset (N~335) shows kappa=0.42. This means the annotators themselves cannot reliably agree on what constitutes stagnation, which undermines the gold set as a validation instrument. With only 52 three-annotator windows (Set A), the IAA estimate itself has wide confidence intervals. The gold set comparison of GB (0.877) vs. TWV Probe (0.854) on SWE-only yields p=0.14---not significant---yet the paper still claims structural dominance. The gold set needed to be 3-5x larger and have higher agreement to support the paper's claims.\n\n- **W3: The \"zero-shot dominance\" claim compares apples to oranges.** The headline \"+16.8pp over best LLM judge\" compares structural GB cross-agent on OH (0.967, silver-evaluated) against Claude 5-shot-CoT on SWE in-domain (0.799, also silver-evaluated). This is not a matched comparison: the structural model is evaluated cross-agent on 150K OH windows while the LLM is evaluated in-domain on 1,824 SWE windows. The paper acknowledges the LLM was not run on OH (\"---\" in Table 1), but headlines the comparison anyway. A fair comparison would require running the LLM judge on OH windows. The \"+33.4pp over probes\" comparison (0.952 vs. 0.618) is matched-scope but the 0.952 is the matched-scope structural number, not the cross-agent 0.967---still, this comparison is less problematic since both are evaluated on the same test set.\n\n- **W4: Outcome backpropagation (lambda_3) is a privileged signal that cannot be used at deployment time.** The paper acknowledges this in the limitations but does not sufficiently address the implications for the headline claims. lambda_3 contributes 33.5% of conf>=2 labels. Removing it drops performance by 7.7pp. The 2-LF deployable configuration (without lambda_3 and without cmd_rep on OH) collapses to 2.81% coverage cross-agent (Table 5 in the task description). This means the \"deployable\" version of this system covers almost no windows on a new agent. The paper buries this critical finding rather than confronting it head-on.\n\n- **W5: The probe comparison is unfair due to evaluation scope mismatch.** Table 1 footnote (a) reveals that the probe is evaluated on a 15% test split (N=6,122) while structural models are evaluated on ALL OH windows (N=150,652). This is a 24.6x difference in evaluation set size. The structural models see windows that the probe was trained on (in the fine-tuned setting) or that overlap with its training distribution. The paper should have restricted structural model evaluation to the same N=6,122 test split for a fair head-to-head.\n\n- **W6: The paper evolved from a failed intervention study.** The review context reveals that the original Claim 4 (early termination as a practical intervention) failed: no k achieves >15% savings with >90% task preservation, the best operating point saves only 7.6% compute with 18.5% false termination, and 43.5% of \"stagnation tails\" contain recovery. The paper pivoted to a \"3-claim detection paper\" but the ghost of the intervention story lingers in the framing (\"compute waste,\" \"deployment trade-off\"). The paper would be more honest as \"we can detect stagnation but cannot usefully act on it.\"\n\n- **W7: Only two agents from the same domain tested.** Both SWE-Agent and OpenHands operate on SWE-bench (GitHub issue resolution). Cross-agent transfer within a single domain and benchmark is a weaker claim than the paper implies. The agents share the same task distribution, success criteria, and output format. True generalization would require testing on web navigation, data analysis, or multi-agent systems.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gold-silver concordance:** What is the agreement rate between silver labels and gold labels on the 505-window gold set? If silver labels are 80% accurate against gold (implied by MV's 0.798 gold F1), then all silver-evaluated metrics in Tables 1-5 have ~20% label noise. How do you reconcile headline claims (0.967) with gold-validated performance (0.839)?\n\n2. **LLM judge on OpenHands:** Why was the LLM judge not evaluated on OH windows? This is the only setting where circularity is broken, yet the most expensive baseline is absent. Even a subsample evaluation would strengthen the comparison.\n\n3. **2-LF deployable coverage collapse:** Table 5 shows 2-LF conf>=2 coverage drops to 2.81% on OH cross-agent. Given that lambda_3 requires outcome labels (unavailable at inference), what is the actual deployable coverage of this system on a new agent?\n\n4. **Stagnation definition validity:** With kappa=0.39, annotators disagree on ~30% of windows. Have you considered that \"semantic stagnation\" may not be a well-defined binary construct? Could a continuous progress score be more appropriate?\n\n5. **Feature overlap interpretation:** The LF-overlap features (11) outperform the full set (30) cross-agent by 1.0pp. Does this not suggest that the structural classifier is simply learning a better version of the LF voting function, rather than detecting stagnation per se?\n\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **LLM judge evaluation on OpenHands.** The paper's headline comparison (structural 0.967 vs. LLM 0.799) is cross-domain vs. in-domain. Running even Claude zero-shot on a subsample of OH would establish whether the LLM's cross-agent transfer is comparable to the structural model's.\n\n- **Gold-evaluated cross-agent transfer.** The gold set should include sufficient OH windows with gold labels to evaluate cross-agent GB performance against human ground truth, not just silver labels. The current gold set has OH windows but the probe cannot be evaluated on them due to \"missing OH embeddings\"---this should have been fixed.\n\n- **Ablation of lambda_3 on cross-agent transfer.** The paper reports that removing lambda_3 costs 7.7pp in-domain but does not report the cross-agent transfer performance of a 2-LF (cmd_rep + file_diff only) structural model. This is the only practically deployable configuration.\n\n- **Stagnation-conditioned task outcome analysis.** If structural features detect stagnation at 0.839 gold F1, does acting on this detection (e.g., early termination, strategy reset) actually improve task completion rates? Without downstream task impact, detection accuracy is necessary but not sufficient for the claimed practical value.\n\n- **Calibration analysis.** The structural classifiers output probabilities. Are these well-calibrated? A reliability diagram would be informative, especially given the silver label noise.\n\n- **Sensitivity to window size k.** The ablation (Table 3 appendix) only tests k in {3,5,7} for the probe. The structural features should also be ablated across window sizes, since the LFs are computed at the window level and k=5 was co-designed with the LFs.\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper title in main.tex (\"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\") differs from the title given in the review prompt. The authors should pick one.\n- Table 1 uses bold/underline conventions but the in-domain column shows both RF and GB at 0.9951 as bold, while the cross-agent columns bold different models. The convention is inconsistent.\n- The label encoding (0=stagnation, 1=progress) is counter-intuitive for a \"stagnation detection\" paper where stagnation is the positive class. Section 3.1 defines this but subsequent text switches between conventions (the error analysis in the appendix explicitly notes \"stagnation-positive convention\").\n- \"Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\" and \"Claude Sonnet 4.6\" and \"GPT-5.5\" are used but only Qwen3 is cited. The other models should have citations or version identifiers.\n- The claim that structural features require \"<5ms per window\" is not benchmarked or cited. A simple timing table would suffice.\n- The GPT-5.5 evaluation has 22.4% parse failures (408/1,824), making it an unreliable baseline. The paper computes F1 over only valid outputs, which inflates the score.\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant problem (detecting when LLM agents are stuck) and demonstrates commendable transparency about circularity. The weak supervision methodology is sound, the feature overlap ablation is well-designed, and the negative label model result is genuinely useful.\n\nHowever, the paper's headline claims do not survive scrutiny against its own evidence. The central tension is: all impressive numbers (0.967, +33.4pp, +16.8pp) are silver-evaluated, while gold-validated performance (0.839, +4.2pp over MV) is modest with low annotator agreement (kappa=0.39). The \"zero-shot dominance\" comparison is not apples-to-apples (cross-agent structural vs. in-domain LLM). The practically deployable configuration (2-LF without outcome labels) collapses to near-zero coverage on a new agent. The paper reads as \"structural features can reconstruct labeling functions well, and this reconstruction transfers across agents\"---a less exciting but more accurate framing than \"structural features dominate stagnation detection.\"\n\nThe work has the ingredients of a solid empirical study but needs: (1) a much larger gold set with higher agreement to anchor the headline claims, (2) matched-scope comparisons throughout (especially running LLM judges on OH), (3) honest confrontation of the deployability gap (lambda_3 requires outcomes, 2-LF collapses cross-agent), and (4) reframing claims to match what the gold evidence actually supports. As currently written, the gap between advertised and validated performance is too large for a top venue.\n\n### 8. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I have read the full paper including appendices, verified the experimental setup against the review context, and checked internal consistency of reported numbers. I am confident in my assessment of the circularity, gold set limitations, and comparison fairness issues. My uncertainty is around whether the cross-agent transfer result (0.967) genuinely reflects stagnation detection rather than LF reconstruction---the feature overlap ablation provides evidence for the former, but the gold set is too small to be definitive."}],"totalDurationMs":141135,"totalTokens":51446,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":679,"cache_read_input_tokens":47222,"output_tokens":3542,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":679},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3542,"cache_read_input_tokens":47222,"cache_creation_input_tokens":679,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":679,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a5a3401a60941d030","tool_use_id":"toolu_01KsWUhu5SM48r8Ajmu6byUq","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":49101,"tool_uses":16,"duration_ms":144700},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"35a9254f-07a2-4654-bad6-3d856ee0b5f1","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Egcn8t37f2wkycCANvoSfD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LCPZewvKrstNhJ3ancw6BX","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies \"semantic stagnation\" detection in LLM coding agents, proposing a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) to generate silver labels over 205K windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands. It systematically compares structural features, a GRU probe, and LLM judges, finding that lightweight structural features dominate in zero-data cross-agent transfer while the probe surpasses them with fine-tuning. Secondary contributions include a temporal dynamics analysis of labeling functions and a negative result showing five label models fail to beat majority vote.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Practically relevant problem.** Semantic stagnation -- where agents appear productive but are stuck -- is a real and underexplored failure mode distinct from simple repetition loops. The paper correctly identifies that existing cycle detectors miss this.\n\n- **Honest treatment of circularity.** The paper is admirably transparent about the circular evaluation problem (structural features reconstructing LFs that produced the labels). The cross-agent evaluation design (train on SWE-Agent, test on OpenHands) is a legitimate and well-motivated way to break this circularity. The feature overlap ablation (Section 5.2) and instance isolation analysis add rigor.\n\n- **Thorough experimental coverage for a short paper.** The paper packs substantial empirical content: main results, cross-agent transfer, robustness ablations, temporal dynamics, label quality analysis, TLM negative results, gold set validation, error analysis, and LLM judge variants. The appendix is well-organized.\n\n- **Useful deployment trade-off framing.** The finding that structural features win in zero-data settings but probes overtake with fine-tuning is a genuinely useful characterization for practitioners. The data efficiency result (GB 0.987 at 10% data) is practically significant.\n\n- **Negative TLM result is informative.** Testing five label models and finding none improve over majority vote in the 3-LF regime is a useful data point for the weak supervision community, with a clear mechanistic explanation (insufficient inter-LF conflict).\n\n- **The cmd_rep coverage analysis is insightful.** Showing that a high-precision, low-recall LF contributes value through coverage expansion rather than label refinement is a non-obvious finding with implications for LF design.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited conceptual novelty -- the core contribution is feature engineering with heuristic labels.** Strip away the weak supervision framing and the paper reduces to: (1) define three heuristic rules for labeling agent stagnation, (2) majority-vote them, (3) train gradient boosted trees on hand-crafted features. Each component is standard. The three LFs -- command repetition, file modification frequency, and outcome backpropagation -- are straightforward heuristics that any practitioner monitoring agent logs would consider. The paper does not introduce a new algorithm, a new architecture, or a new theoretical insight. It is a competent empirical study, but the novelty bar for a top venue is high.\n\n- **The \"weak supervision\" framing overpromises relative to what is delivered.** The paper invokes the data programming paradigm (Ratner et al.) but uses it in its simplest possible form: three hand-written LFs + majority vote. The temporal label models all fail. FlyingSquid and Snorkel MeTaL collapse. The paper effectively demonstrates that the sophisticated weak supervision machinery is unnecessary here -- majority vote over 3 heuristics suffices. This undermines the methodological positioning: the paper is not advancing weak supervision for agent monitoring so much as showing that basic heuristics work.\n\n- **Cross-agent \"transfer\" is tested on exactly one pair of agents, both on the same benchmark.** SWE-Agent and OpenHands both operate on SWE-bench GitHub issues. The paper claims \"zero-shot cross-coding-agent transfer\" but this is transfer between two agents solving the same task distribution. True generalization to different agent architectures (web agents, data analysis agents, multi-agent systems) or different task distributions is untested. The Limitations section acknowledges this, but the title and abstract still overstate the generality.\n\n- **The 20pp circularity gap (in-domain 0.995 vs gold 0.839 on GB; 0.995 vs 0.792 for MV) is severe and underweighted in the narrative.** The gold set reveals that the silver labels -- the evaluation standard for all main tables -- are substantially misaligned with human judgment. When the paper's primary metric shows 0.995 F1 but gold validation shows 0.839, the reader must question every silver-evaluated number. The paper acknowledges circularity but still foregrounds the silver numbers in the abstract, introduction, and conclusion. A more honest framing would lead with the gold set results and use silver metrics as secondary.\n\n- **Gold set is underpowered and has concerning IAA.** Fleiss kappa = 0.39 is \"fair\" agreement at best. The gold set has only 505 windows (52 with all three annotators), which is small for validating claims over 205K windows. The SWE-only subset for probe evaluation is ~335 windows. The paper should be more cautious in how much weight it places on gold set conclusions.\n\n- **Outcome backpropagation (lambda_3) is a privileged signal unavailable at inference time.** This is acknowledged in Limitations but not sufficiently addressed in the main text. A labeling function that requires knowing the task outcome cannot be used in a real-time monitoring system. Since lambda_3 contributes 33.5% of conf>=2 labels, the silver label quality depends substantially on a signal that breaks the deployment scenario the paper motivates. The 2-LF configuration (without lambda_3 and without cmd_rep) collapses cross-agent, highlighting this fragility.\n\n- **Related work is organized topically but has notable gaps (detailed below).** With ~40 references in the bibliography, the paper has improved from an earlier version, but several important related areas remain underdiscussed.\n\n- **No figures in the main paper body (aside from referenced pipeline/scaling figures).** The paper is extremely table-heavy. A well-designed figure showing, e.g., example stagnation trajectories, the temporal dynamics of LFs, or the cross-agent transfer gap would significantly improve readability and help the reader build intuition for the problem.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On novelty:** What specific technical contribution does this paper make beyond \"apply standard weak supervision + gradient boosted trees to agent monitoring\"? If the answer is \"the finding that structural features suffice,\" how is this different from the well-known result that hand-crafted features often beat learned representations for narrow classification tasks?\n\n2. **On the gold set:** Given kappa=0.39, how confident are you that \"stagnation\" has a stable ground truth? Have you considered that the moderate IAA reflects fundamental ambiguity in the concept rather than annotator noise? If so, what does it mean to claim 0.967 F1 on a concept that humans agree on only fairly?\n\n3. **On deployment:** You motivate the work with real-time monitoring (<5ms per window), but lambda_3 requires task outcomes. In a realistic deployment, you would have only lambda_1 and lambda_2. What is the expected performance with only deployment-available LFs? The 2-LF result in Table 5 (0.682 macro-F1) suggests a dramatic degradation.\n\n4. **On the cross-agent claim:** Both agents operate on SWE-bench. Would you expect structural features to transfer to, say, a web navigation agent or a data analysis agent? If not, how should the reader scope the generality claim?\n\n5. **On the negative TLM result:** You attribute the failure to LF quantity (3 LFs). Have you verified this hypothesis by, e.g., adding synthetic LFs with controlled noise to see if label models start helping at 5, 7, or 10 LFs?\n\n6. **On label version bias:** You report that a \"version mismatch in OH embedding vectors\" caused stagnation rate to shift from 6.56% to 21.4%. This is presented as a finding about \"label quality as transfer bottleneck,\" but it reads more like a bug fix. Can you clarify what exactly was wrong and why this constitutes a scientific contribution rather than an engineering correction?\n\n## 5. Related Work Gaps\n\n- **Anomaly detection in sequential data.** The paper's problem -- detecting anomalous behavioral patterns in action sequences -- is closely related to time-series anomaly detection. The Isolation Forest reference is insufficient. Missing: deep anomaly detection surveys (Pang et al., 2021, \"Deep Learning for Anomaly Detection: A Review\"), OC-SVM for sequential data, autoencoders for trajectory anomaly (Park et al., 2018). TrajAD is cited but not compared against.\n\n- **Process mining and conformance checking.** Van der Aalst (2016) is cited once in passing, but the paper's structural features (repetition ratios, edit distances, command distributions) are essentially process mining features. The connection to conformance checking -- detecting deviations from expected process models -- deserves discussion.\n\n- **Agent benchmarking beyond SWE-bench.** The paper cites SWE-bench but ignores AgentBench (Liu et al., 2023), MINT (Wang et al., 2024), and other multi-domain agent benchmarks where stagnation detection would be relevant. This limits the positioning.\n\n- **Runtime verification and monitoring.** The real-time monitoring framing connects to runtime verification literature (Leucker & Schallhart, 2009; Bartocci et al., 2018). The paper's structural features are essentially runtime monitors for behavioral properties -- this connection is not drawn.\n\n- **Active learning / human-in-the-loop for agent oversight.** The paper's deployment trade-off (structural for zero-data, probe with fine-tuning) naturally connects to active learning strategies for efficiently acquiring labels. This is not discussed.\n\n- **Contemporary agent failure work.** DebugBench (Tian et al., 2024), AgentTrek (Xu et al., 2025), and other recent agent debugging/trajectory analysis works are not cited.\n\n- **Feature importance / interpretability.** The paper relies heavily on GB feature importance but does not connect to the interpretable ML literature (SHAP, LIME) or discuss whether the structural features provide interpretable failure diagnoses.\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The submitted title \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\" uses a question framing for a question the paper clearly answers affirmatively. Consider whether this is misleading vs. the more direct \"Structural Features Dominate...\" formulation.\n\n- Table 1 footnotes are dense and hard to parse. Consider moving some information to the main text.\n\n- The paper uses \"cross-coding-agent\" throughout when \"cross-agent\" would be clearer and more standard.\n\n- Section 3.1 defines y=0 as stagnation and y=1 as progress, but the error analysis (Appendix C.8) uses \"stagnation-positive convention.\" This convention inconsistency should be clarified.\n\n- The paper could benefit from a running example showing what a stagnation window looks like vs. a progress window, to ground the reader's intuition.\n\n- Cost comparison with LLM judges ($11.66-$40.56 per test set) is useful but the structural feature extraction cost is not quantified beyond \"<5ms per window.\"\n\n## 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThis is a thorough and honest empirical study of a relevant problem. The authors deserve credit for transparent handling of circularity, comprehensive ablations, and informative negative results. However, the paper falls short of the novelty threshold for a top ML venue for several reasons:\n\n(1) The core technical contribution -- three heuristic labeling functions + majority vote + gradient boosted trees on hand-crafted features -- uses only standard components with no methodological innovation. The \"finding\" that structural features beat learned representations in low-data transfer is well-established in ML.\n\n(2) The weak supervision framing is undercut by the paper's own results: all sophisticated label models fail, and majority vote over 3 heuristics is all you need. The paper effectively argues against its own methodological framework.\n\n(3) The evaluation relies predominantly on silver labels that have a ~16pp gap with human gold annotations (0.839 vs 0.995 in-domain), yet the main claims are stated in terms of the silver metrics. The gold set is too small (505 windows, kappa=0.39) to serve as a reliable corrective.\n\n(4) Cross-agent transfer is demonstrated on a single agent pair over the same task benchmark, limiting the generality claim.\n\n(5) One of three labeling functions (lambda_3) requires privileged information unavailable at deployment, and the deployment-available configuration (2-LF) degrades substantially.\n\nThe paper would be strengthened by: (a) a larger and higher-IAA gold set as the primary evaluation; (b) demonstration on at least one non-SE agent domain; (c) a more modest framing as an empirical analysis rather than a methodological contribution; (d) deeper engagement with anomaly detection and process mining literature.\n\n## 8. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I am confident in this assessment. I have expertise in weak supervision, agent evaluation, and ML methodology. I have read the paper thoroughly including all appendices. My main uncertainty is whether the practical utility of the deployment trade-off finding is sufficient to overcome the novelty concerns -- reasonable reviewers could weigh this differently.","project":"agent_progress_probe","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_review","overall_score":"3 - Weak Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":48113,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KsWUhu5SM48r8Ajmu6byUq","session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"b293723a-db25-4f95-ae09-9a60fc50fe29"}
