{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AmZdCqJm6dApZFeXSZ34sy","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n# Review: Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes a weak supervision pipeline for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories on SWE-bench, combining three heuristic labeling functions (exact-match repetition, edit-distance similarity, TF-IDF cosine similarity) with majority vote aggregation and a Gradient Boosted classifier over hand-crafted structural features. The authors report a 16.8 percentage-point improvement over an LLM-as-judge baseline on silver-label evaluation and investigate cross-agent transfer between two agents. The paper frames itself as a methodology study for deployment-grade agent oversight, but the core contribution reduces to showing that simple string-matching heuristics and standard classifiers outperform a naive GPT-4o judge on this particular binary detection task.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Practically relevant problem.** Detecting when an LLM agent is stuck in repetitive loops is a real deployment concern. Early termination of stagnating agents saves compute, and the paper's framing around this use case is well-motivated.\n\n- **Systematic negative result on label models.** The finding that Snorkel-style label models (including those with learned dependency structures) fail to improve over naive majority vote is clearly documented and useful for practitioners considering weak supervision for similar tasks. Table 3 makes this concrete.\n\n- **Honest reporting of gold-label degradation.** The paper does not hide the large gap between silver-label performance (F1 ~0.995) and gold-label performance (F1 ~0.839). This is more transparent than many weak supervision papers that only report on their own silver labels.\n\n- **Ablation coverage.** The feature ablation (Table 8), threshold sensitivity analysis, and cross-agent transfer experiment show effort toward thoroughness. The structural-input contribution analysis identifying which features drive performance is informative.\n\n- **Reproducibility intent.** The authors state that code and data will be released, and the labeling functions are simple enough to reimplement from the paper alone.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **No methodological novelty.** The pipeline is: (1) write 3 rule-based heuristic labeling functions, (2) combine them with majority vote, (3) train a Gradient Boosted tree on hand-crafted features using the majority-vote labels. Every component is entirely standard. The labeling functions are straightforward string-matching rules (exact match, edit distance > threshold, TF-IDF cosine > threshold). Majority vote is the simplest possible aggregation. Gradient Boosting on tabular features is a textbook classifier. The paper does not introduce any new algorithm, architecture, loss function, or training procedure. Applying known tools to a new domain is acceptable if the domain insights are deep, but here the insight is essentially \"repetitive text can be detected by string matching,\" which is not surprising.\n\n- **The weak supervision framing undermines itself.** The paper's title and framing center on weak supervision, yet the core experimental finding is that weak supervision's distinctive contribution — the label model — provides no benefit. Majority vote (the trivial baseline for combining LFs) matches or exceeds all learned label models (Table 3). This means the \"weak supervision study\" demonstrates that the weak supervision machinery is unnecessary for this problem. The paper would be more honest titled as \"Rule-Based Heuristics for Stagnation Detection\" but that would expose how thin the contribution is.\n\n- **Silver-label evaluation is circular and dominates the narrative.** The headline number (16.8pp gain, Table 1) is measured against silver labels generated by the same labeling functions used to train the classifier. This is methodologically problematic: the classifier is optimizing for agreement with the LFs, so high silver-label performance primarily measures how well GB memorizes the LF outputs, not how well it detects actual stagnation. The gold-label evaluation (Table 5) tells the real story — F1 of 0.839 with precision 0.870 and recall 0.811 — which is decent but far less impressive. Yet the paper leads with silver numbers in the abstract and introduction, which is misleading.\n\n- **Gold evaluation set is critically underpowered.** N=505 with inter-annotator kappa of 0.39 (Table 6) is weak. A kappa of 0.39 falls in the \"fair agreement\" range by standard interpretation (Landis & Koch, 1977), meaning annotators disagree on a substantial fraction of cases. With only 505 samples and a noisy ground truth, the gold-label F1 estimates have wide confidence intervals. The bootstrap CIs reported (e.g., [0.810, 0.866]) span 5.6 points, which is large enough that many of the claimed comparisons between methods are not statistically distinguishable. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address it — a larger, higher-agreement gold set is needed before any of the gold-label conclusions can be trusted.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer is minimally tested.** The transfer experiment (Experiment 6) uses exactly one agent pair (MoatlessTools to Agentless) on the same underlying task distribution (SWE-bench Verified). This tells us almost nothing about generalization. Both agents operate on the same benchmark, receive the same task descriptions, and produce outputs in similar formats. Testing on a truly different agent architecture, a different task domain (e.g., web navigation, tool use, multi-step reasoning), or even a different code-repair benchmark would be necessary to support claims about transfer. The current experiment is closer to a within-distribution held-out split than a genuine transfer study.\n\n- **Lambda_3 (TF-IDF cosine) is privileged and unavailable at deployment.** Lambda_3 requires computing TF-IDF vectors over a corpus of agent trajectories. At deployment time, the TF-IDF vocabulary and IDF weights must come from somewhere — either precomputed on training data (introducing distribution assumptions) or computed online (requiring a buffer of trajectories). The paper does not discuss this deployment constraint. More critically, when Lambda_3 is removed (the 2-LF setting), performance degrades to the point where the system may not be practically useful. This means the \"zero-shot\" framing is somewhat misleading — the system depends on a corpus-derived feature that requires offline preparation.\n\n- **The negative TLM result, while honest, is narrow and incremental.** Showing that a trivially prompted GPT-4o judge underperforms string-matching heuristics on repetition detection is not surprising. The TLM baseline uses what appears to be a simple binary prompt without chain-of-thought, few-shot examples, structured output parsing, or any of the techniques known to improve LLM classification. The +8.7pp gain from adding chain-of-thought (Table 7) hints that a properly engineered LLM judge could close much of the gap, but the paper does not pursue this. The negative result characterizes a weak baseline, not the frontier of LLM-as-judge capability.\n\n- **\"Semantic stagnation\" may not be a novel concept.** The paper defines semantic stagnation as repeated or near-repeated actions in a sequential trace. This is essentially cycle detection or anomaly detection in sequential data, which has decades of literature in process mining, software engineering (infinite loop detection), and time-series analysis. The paper does not engage with this prior work or explain what distinguishes \"semantic stagnation\" from these well-studied phenomena.\n\n- **No figures.** The paper contains only tables — no architecture diagram, no visualization of stagnation patterns, no reliability diagram, no feature importance plot, no example trajectory. This makes the paper harder to read and less compelling than it could be. A single figure showing an example stagnating trajectory with the LF activations overlaid would do more to build intuition than several tables of numbers.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On silver vs. gold evaluation:** If you had to present only one set of results, would you choose the silver-label or gold-label numbers? If gold, why does the abstract lead with the silver-label 16.8pp number?\n\n2. **On the label model negative result:** Have you investigated *why* the label models fail? With only 3 binary LFs that have high pairwise correlation (they all detect repetition), the label model may simply not have enough signal diversity to learn meaningful dependencies. Would adding LFs that capture different aspects of stagnation (e.g., lack of progress toward goal, increasing error rates, decreasing action diversity) change this result?\n\n3. **On Lambda_3 deployment:** How do you envision the TF-IDF component being deployed in practice? Is the IDF vocabulary fixed from training, or recomputed? What happens when the agent's output distribution shifts from the SWE-bench trajectories used to build the vocabulary?\n\n4. **On the gold set kappa:** A kappa of 0.39 suggests that the annotators had substantively different interpretations of \"stagnation.\" Did you analyze the disagreement cases? Were they concentrated in particular trajectory types (e.g., near-misses where the agent makes slight variations on a failing approach)?\n\n5. **On cross-agent transfer:** Why was only one agent pair tested? Were there practical constraints, or was this a deliberate choice? What do you predict would happen on an agent operating in a fundamentally different domain (e.g., WebArena, InterCode)?\n\n6. **On the LLM judge baseline:** Have you tried more sophisticated prompting strategies (few-shot, structured output, self-consistency) for the GPT-4o judge? The 8.7pp CoT improvement suggests significant headroom remains.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Related Work Gaps\n\nThe paper cites only **10 references**, which is far too few for a venue like ICML/NeurIPS/EMNLP. For context, most accepted papers in these venues cite 30-60 references. The sparse bibliography creates the impression that the authors have not adequately surveyed the field. Specific gaps:\n\n- **Anomaly detection in sequential data.** The core task — detecting anomalous repetitive patterns in a sequence of actions — is well-studied in time-series anomaly detection (Chandola et al., 2009), sequence mining (Agrawal & Srikant, 1995), and log analysis (He et al., 2016; Du et al., 2017 — DeepLog). The paper makes no connection to this literature, missing an opportunity to position the contribution and potentially adopt stronger baselines.\n\n- **Process mining.** Detecting cycles and repetitive patterns in event logs is a core problem in process mining (van der Aalst, 2016). Process discovery algorithms, conformance checking, and directly-follows graphs are all relevant. The paper does not cite or discuss this field.\n\n- **Runtime verification and monitoring.** The problem of monitoring an executing agent for undesirable behaviors connects to runtime verification (Leucker & Schallhart, 2009), where properties are checked against execution traces. This is a natural framing for stagnation detection that the paper ignores.\n\n- **Agent benchmarks beyond SWE-bench.** The paper operates exclusively on SWE-bench but does not discuss other agent benchmarks where stagnation detection would be relevant: WebArena (Zhou et al., 2023), InterCode (Yang et al., 2023), AgentBench (Liu et al., 2023), ToolBench (Qin et al., 2023). Even a discussion of why SWE-bench was chosen over alternatives would strengthen the paper.\n\n- **DebugBench and AgentTrek.** DebugBench (Tian et al., 2024) studies LLM debugging capabilities and could provide complementary trajectory data. AgentTrek (Xu et al., 2024) provides annotated agent trajectories for web tasks. Neither is cited.\n\n- **Active learning for annotation.** Given the acknowledged weakness of the gold evaluation set, active learning approaches for efficiently collecting high-quality labels (Settles, 2009) are directly relevant. The paper does not discuss how to improve the gold set.\n\n- **Programmatic labeling and data-centric AI.** Beyond Snorkel, the broader data-centric AI movement (Zha et al., 2023) and programmatic labeling frameworks (e.g., Flywheel, Nemo) provide context for the LF-based approach. The related work section gives only a surface treatment of weak supervision.\n\n- **Early stopping and curriculum learning.** The compute-saving motivation connects to work on early stopping criteria, patience-based training termination, and curriculum learning, none of which are discussed.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract states \"zero-shot\" but the GB classifier is trained on silver-labeled data from the target distribution. Only the LFs themselves are zero-shot (they use no labeled examples). This conflation is confusing.\n\n- Table 2 lists LF specifications but does not report per-LF precision and recall on the gold set, only on silver. Per-LF gold-set performance would be far more informative.\n\n- The paper mentions \"conf >= 2\" filtering but the notation is introduced without formal definition. What exactly does confidence level mean in the majority vote context with 3 LFs?\n\n- Section ordering is somewhat unusual — the limitations section appearing before experiments would be more standard as a subsection of the discussion.\n\n- Several tables could be consolidated. Tables 1, 3, 4, and 5 all report classifier performance under slightly different conditions and could be unified into a single comprehensive table with clear column headers.\n\n- The writing occasionally slips into promotional language (\"dominate,\" \"deployment-grade\") that is not supported by the evidence. The gold-label F1 of 0.839 is reasonable but not dominant, and deployment-grade systems typically require more extensive validation than N=505 with kappa=0.39.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThis paper addresses a practical problem — detecting when LLM agents are stuck — but does not provide sufficient novelty or depth to merit acceptance at a top venue. The pipeline is an assembly of standard components (string-matching heuristics, majority vote, Gradient Boosting) with no new methodological contribution. The paper's own findings undermine its weak supervision framing: the label model adds nothing over majority vote, meaning the Snorkel-inspired machinery is shown to be unnecessary. The headline results are measured on silver labels that are circular with the training signal; the more meaningful gold-label results are less impressive and measured on an underpowered evaluation set with low inter-annotator agreement. Cross-agent transfer is tested on a single agent pair within the same benchmark distribution. The related work coverage is thin (10 citations), missing several directly relevant fields. The negative result on LLM-as-judge, while honest, characterizes a weak baseline rather than the frontier of LLM classification capability.\n\nThe paper tries to contribute simultaneously to weak supervision methodology, LLM agent monitoring, and the LLM-vs-heuristic debate, but does not go deep enough in any direction. A more focused version — either a thorough empirical study of stagnation patterns across multiple agent types and benchmarks, or a methodological contribution introducing new LFs/aggregation strategies that actually outperform majority vote — would be stronger.\n\nI would encourage the authors to: (1) expand the gold evaluation set substantially and improve annotator agreement, (2) test on multiple agent architectures and task domains, (3) engage with the broader literature on sequential anomaly detection and process mining, (4) either demonstrate that weak supervision machinery adds value or honestly reframe as a heuristic-based approach, and (5) include at least basic visualizations of the phenomena they study.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I am confident in my assessment of the novelty and positioning issues. I have familiarity with weak supervision (Snorkel and successors), LLM agent evaluation, and sequential anomaly detection. My main uncertainty is whether the practical utility of the tool — even without novelty — might be valued differently by other reviewers focused on applications. However, even for an applications contribution, the evaluation is insufficiently rigorous (small gold set, single benchmark, one transfer pair)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5957f936b6fea706 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5957f936b6fea706' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 19086\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 97301</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62a285b4-2e65-40ae-891e-0aaa22cfb53a","uuid":"370d11b6-ac8b-4f2d-a1b9-38bece4072ba","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:49:17.929Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Please provide the complete, detailed Reviewer 2 (R2) review for the paper \"Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\". \n\nYou are R2, focused on novelty, positioning, and related work. Your score is 3 (Weak Reject), confidence 4/5.\n\nGenerate the FULL review with all sections:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Related work gaps\n6. Minor issues\n7. Overall assessment (score + justification)\n8. Confidence\n\nKey points to cover in detail:\n- Core pipeline (3 heuristic LFs + MV + GB on hand-crafted features) is standard components, no methodological novelty\n- Weak supervision framing is self-defeating: paper shows all label models fail, MV suffices\n- Silver-label evaluation dominates with ~16pp gap vs gold (0.995 vs 0.839)\n- Gold set underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39)\n- Cross-agent transfer only tested on one agent pair over same SWE-bench distribution\n- Lambda_3 privileged and unavailable at deployment; 2-LF collapses\n- Related work gaps: anomaly detection in sequential data, process mining, agent benchmarks beyond SWE-bench, runtime verification, active learning for agent oversight, DebugBench/AgentTrek\n- Only 10 references is far too few\n- Negative TLM result is incremental -- well-characterized but narrow scope (3 binary LFs)\n- The \"semantic stagnation\" concept itself may not be novel over existing cycle/anomaly detection\n- Paper tries to be too many things at once without excelling at any\n- No figures in the paper (all tables)\n\nProvide the full review text with specific evidence from the paper for each point.","agentId":"a5957f936b6fea706","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n# Review: Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Stagnation Detection in LLM Agent Trajectories: A Weak Supervision Study\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper proposes a weak supervision pipeline for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories on SWE-bench, combining three heuristic labeling functions (exact-match repetition, edit-distance similarity, TF-IDF cosine similarity) with majority vote aggregation and a Gradient Boosted classifier over hand-crafted structural features. The authors report a 16.8 percentage-point improvement over an LLM-as-judge baseline on silver-label evaluation and investigate cross-agent transfer between two agents. The paper frames itself as a methodology study for deployment-grade agent oversight, but the core contribution reduces to showing that simple string-matching heuristics and standard classifiers outperform a naive GPT-4o judge on this particular binary detection task.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Practically relevant problem.** Detecting when an LLM agent is stuck in repetitive loops is a real deployment concern. Early termination of stagnating agents saves compute, and the paper's framing around this use case is well-motivated.\n\n- **Systematic negative result on label models.** The finding that Snorkel-style label models (including those with learned dependency structures) fail to improve over naive majority vote is clearly documented and useful for practitioners considering weak supervision for similar tasks. Table 3 makes this concrete.\n\n- **Honest reporting of gold-label degradation.** The paper does not hide the large gap between silver-label performance (F1 ~0.995) and gold-label performance (F1 ~0.839). This is more transparent than many weak supervision papers that only report on their own silver labels.\n\n- **Ablation coverage.** The feature ablation (Table 8), threshold sensitivity analysis, and cross-agent transfer experiment show effort toward thoroughness. The structural-input contribution analysis identifying which features drive performance is informative.\n\n- **Reproducibility intent.** The authors state that code and data will be released, and the labeling functions are simple enough to reimplement from the paper alone.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **No methodological novelty.** The pipeline is: (1) write 3 rule-based heuristic labeling functions, (2) combine them with majority vote, (3) train a Gradient Boosted tree on hand-crafted features using the majority-vote labels. Every component is entirely standard. The labeling functions are straightforward string-matching rules (exact match, edit distance > threshold, TF-IDF cosine > threshold). Majority vote is the simplest possible aggregation. Gradient Boosting on tabular features is a textbook classifier. The paper does not introduce any new algorithm, architecture, loss function, or training procedure. Applying known tools to a new domain is acceptable if the domain insights are deep, but here the insight is essentially \"repetitive text can be detected by string matching,\" which is not surprising.\n\n- **The weak supervision framing undermines itself.** The paper's title and framing center on weak supervision, yet the core experimental finding is that weak supervision's distinctive contribution — the label model — provides no benefit. Majority vote (the trivial baseline for combining LFs) matches or exceeds all learned label models (Table 3). This means the \"weak supervision study\" demonstrates that the weak supervision machinery is unnecessary for this problem. The paper would be more honest titled as \"Rule-Based Heuristics for Stagnation Detection\" but that would expose how thin the contribution is.\n\n- **Silver-label evaluation is circular and dominates the narrative.** The headline number (16.8pp gain, Table 1) is measured against silver labels generated by the same labeling functions used to train the classifier. This is methodologically problematic: the classifier is optimizing for agreement with the LFs, so high silver-label performance primarily measures how well GB memorizes the LF outputs, not how well it detects actual stagnation. The gold-label evaluation (Table 5) tells the real story — F1 of 0.839 with precision 0.870 and recall 0.811 — which is decent but far less impressive. Yet the paper leads with silver numbers in the abstract and introduction, which is misleading.\n\n- **Gold evaluation set is critically underpowered.** N=505 with inter-annotator kappa of 0.39 (Table 6) is weak. A kappa of 0.39 falls in the \"fair agreement\" range by standard interpretation (Landis & Koch, 1977), meaning annotators disagree on a substantial fraction of cases. With only 505 samples and a noisy ground truth, the gold-label F1 estimates have wide confidence intervals. The bootstrap CIs reported (e.g., [0.810, 0.866]) span 5.6 points, which is large enough that many of the claimed comparisons between methods are not statistically distinguishable. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address it — a larger, higher-agreement gold set is needed before any of the gold-label conclusions can be trusted.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer is minimally tested.** The transfer experiment (Experiment 6) uses exactly one agent pair (MoatlessTools to Agentless) on the same underlying task distribution (SWE-bench Verified). This tells us almost nothing about generalization. Both agents operate on the same benchmark, receive the same task descriptions, and produce outputs in similar formats. Testing on a truly different agent architecture, a different task domain (e.g., web navigation, tool use, multi-step reasoning), or even a different code-repair benchmark would be necessary to support claims about transfer. The current experiment is closer to a within-distribution held-out split than a genuine transfer study.\n\n- **Lambda_3 (TF-IDF cosine) is privileged and unavailable at deployment.** Lambda_3 requires computing TF-IDF vectors over a corpus of agent trajectories. At deployment time, the TF-IDF vocabulary and IDF weights must come from somewhere — either precomputed on training data (introducing distribution assumptions) or computed online (requiring a buffer of trajectories). The paper does not discuss this deployment constraint. More critically, when Lambda_3 is removed (the 2-LF setting), performance degrades to the point where the system may not be practically useful. This means the \"zero-shot\" framing is somewhat misleading — the system depends on a corpus-derived feature that requires offline preparation.\n\n- **The negative TLM result, while honest, is narrow and incremental.** Showing that a trivially prompted GPT-4o judge underperforms string-matching heuristics on repetition detection is not surprising